<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/detectionMethod/295539>	ns2:detectionDescription	"\n                  According to SOAR, the following detection techniques may be useful:\n                  \n                     Highly cost effective:\n                     \n                        \n                           Focused Manual Spotcheck - Focused manual analysis of source\n                           Manual Source Code Review (not inspections)\n                        \n                     \n                  \n               " .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/detectionMethod/295632>	ns2:detectionDescription	"\n                  According to SOAR, the following detection techniques may be useful:\n                  \n                     Cost effective for partial coverage:\n                     \n                        \n                           Bytecode Weakness Analysis - including disassembler + source code weakness analysis\n                           Binary Weakness Analysis - including disassembler + source code weakness analysis\n                        \n                     \n                  \n               " .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/detectionMethod/297227>	ns2:detectionDescription	"When Certificate Pinning is being used in a mobile application, consider using a tool such as Spinner [REF-955].  This methodology might be extensible to other technologies." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/detectionMethod/297515>	ns2:detectionDescription	"Set up an untrusted endpoint (e.g. a server) with which the software will connect.  Create a test certificate that uses an invalid hostname but is signed by a trusted CA and provide this certificate from the untrusted endpoint. If the software performs any operations instead of disconnecting and reporting an error, then this indicates that the hostname is not being checked and the test certificate has been accepted." .
