<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/1007280>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"This weakness may occur when characters from various character sets are allowed to be interchanged within a URL, username, email address, etc. without any notification to the user or underlying system being used." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/1022218>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"This weakness is introduced during the coding of an application when the developer does not include the noopener and/or noreferrer value for the rel attribute." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/1022261>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"This weakness is introduced during the design of an application when the architect does not specify that a linked external document should not be able to alter the location of the calling page." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/1037193>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"Optimizations built into the design of the processor can have unintended consequences during the execution of an application." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/1038167>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"Optimizations built into the design of a product can have unintended consequences during execution." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/1039133>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"This issue can be introduced into the automated algorithm itself." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/105206>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"Some applications use the same ActionForm for more than one purpose. In situations like this, some fields may go unused under some action mappings." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/114154>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"REALIZATION: This weakness is caused during implementation of an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/117154>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"REALIZATION: This weakness is caused during implementation of an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/1173158>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"This weakness may occur when developers do not correctly use a provided input validation framework." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/1173199>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"This weakness may occur when software designers choose to not leverage input validation frameworks provided by the source language." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/134264>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"The programmer rarely intends for a format string to be externally-controlled at all. This weakness is frequently introduced in code that constructs log messages, where a constant format string is omitted." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/134357>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"In cases such as localization and internationalization, the language-specific message repositories could be an avenue for exploitation, but the format string issue would be resultant, since attacker control of those repositories would also allow modification of message length, format, and content." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/1351117>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"\n                  There are several ways in which improper string length checking may result in an exploitable condition. All of these, however, involve the introduction of buffer overflow conditions in order to reach an exploitable state.\n                  The first of these issues takes place when the output of a wide or multi-byte character string, string-length function is used as a size for the allocation of memory. While this will result in an output of the number of characters in the string, note that the characters are most likely not a single byte, as they are with standard character strings. So, using the size returned as the size sent to new or malloc and copying the string to this newly allocated memory will result in a buffer overflow.\n                  Another common way these strings are misused involves the mixing of standard string and wide or multi-byte string functions on a single string. Invariably, this mismatched information will result in the creation of a possibly exploitable buffer overflow condition.\n               " .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/138154>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"REALIZATION: This weakness is caused during implementation of an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/150154>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"REALIZATION: This weakness is caused during implementation of an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/15154>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"REALIZATION: This weakness is caused during implementation of an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/15264>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"Setting manipulation vulnerabilities occur when an attacker can control values that govern the behavior of the system, manage specific resources, or in some way affect the functionality of the application." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/179266>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"Since early validation errors usually arise from improperly implemented defensive mechanisms, it is likely that these will be introduced more frequently as secure programming becomes implemented more widely." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/201154>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"REALIZATION: This weakness is caused during implementation of an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/20747>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"\n                  REALIZATION: This weakness is caused during implementation of an architectural security tactic.\n                  If a programmer believes that an attacker cannot modify certain inputs, then the programmer might not perform any input validation at all. For example, in web applications, many programmers believe that cookies and hidden form fields can not be modified from a web browser (CWE-472), although they can be altered using a proxy or a custom program. In a client-server architecture, the programmer might assume that client-side security checks cannot be bypassed, even when a custom client could be written that skips those checks (CWE-602).\n               " .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/208168>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"COMMISSION: This weakness refers to an incorrect design related to an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/209154>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"REALIZATION: This weakness is caused during implementation of an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/210154>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"REALIZATION: This weakness is caused during implementation of an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/211154>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"REALIZATION: This weakness is caused during implementation of an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/211270>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"PHP applications are often targeted for having this issue when the PHP interpreter generates the error outside of the application's control. However, other languages/environments exhibit the same issue." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/212154>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"REALIZATION: This weakness is caused during implementation of an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/214154>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"REALIZATION: This weakness is caused during implementation of an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/219154>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"COMMISSION: This weakness refers to an incorrect design related to an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/219159>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"COMMISSION: This weakness refers to an incorrect design related to an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/220168>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"COMMISSION: This weakness refers to an incorrect design related to an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/223172>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"OMISSION: This weakness is caused by missing a security tactic during the architecture and design phase." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/224154>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"REALIZATION: This weakness is caused during implementation of an architectural security tactic." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/231127>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"This typically occurs in situations when only one value is expected." .
<http://w3id.org/sepses/resource/cwe/modeOfIntroduction/235145>	ns2:modeOfIntroductionNote	"This typically occurs in situations when only one element is expected to be specified." .
